## [fix(context): keep headers staged via header() when c.res is set to a raw Response](https://github.com/honojs/hono/pull/5397)

## Summary

`c.header()` called before `c.res` has ever been read or assigned stages the header on a private `#preparedHeaders` object, since `#res` isn't materialized yet. The `res` setter's merge logic only ever looked at `this.#res.headers`, so it never saw `#preparedHeaders` — any header staged this way was silently lost if the response was later set directly, e.g. a handler returning a raw `Response` instead of going through `c.text()`/`c.json()`/`c.body()`:

```ts
app.use('*', async (c, next) => {
  c.header('x-foo', 'bar')
  await next()
})
app.get('/', () => new Response('hi'))

// GET / -> `x-foo` header is missing
```

The same request keeps the header fine if the handler uses `c.text('hi')` instead, since that path reads `#preparedHeaders` directly (via `#newResponse`). Returning a raw `Response` from a handler is a documented, supported pattern, so this asymmetry is surprising and easy to hit in real apps (e.g. a logging/tracing middleware that sets a header before `next()`, in front of a route that returns a `Response` built by some other library).

## Fix

- The `res` setter now falls back to `#preparedHeaders` when `#res` doesn't exist yet, so those headers get merged into whatever is assigned to `c.res`.
- Because a `res` assignment (including the `c.res = undefined` reset pattern some middleware use to fully discard a downstream response before building a fresh one) always supersedes whatever was staged, `#preparedHeaders` is cleared once the setter runs. This avoids stale staged headers leaking into a later, unrelated response — an existing test (`Overwrite the response from middleware after next()` > `Should not have the custom header`) regressed on my first pass without this and is what led me to add it.

