## [Context, Request, and Middleware Composition | Read OSS](https://readoss.com/en/honojs/hono/context-request-and-middleware-composition)
Published: 2026-08-13T01:56:50.000Z

The `res` setter is where header merging gets sophisticated. When middleware sets headers before the handler creates the response, those headers need to be merged. But `Set-Cookie` headers require special treatment — they can't be merged with `headers.set()` because that overwrites previous cookies:

`src/context.ts#L414-L434`

```
set res(_res: Response | undefined) {
  if (this.#res && _res) {
    _res = createResponseInstance(_res.body, _res)
    for (const [k, v] of this.#res.headers.entries()) {
      if (k === 'content-type') {
        continue
      }
      if (k === 'set-cookie') {
        const cookies = this.#res.headers.getSetCookie()
        _res.headers.delete('set-cookie')
        for (const cookie of cookies) {
          _res.headers.append('set-cookie', cookie)
        }
      } else {
        _res.headers.set(k, v)
      }
    }
  }
  this.#res = _res
  this.finalized = true
}
```

The `getSetCookie()` API returns an array of individual cookie strings, avoiding the concatenation issue that plagued the old `getAll('set-cookie')` approach. Hono deletes the existing set-cookie header, then appends each cookie individually.

> Tip: If you're setting cookies in middleware and in a handler, both will be preserved. But watch out for setting `Content-Type` in middleware — it gets skipped during merge (line 419) because the handler's content type takes priority.

